 ],
 "literal": "Great cool rain's tender wild jasmine's / buds even before turned very red indeed. / Like a deer, I am bewildered, oh friend, at midnight. / Still, if he who is alone hears, once again, / what will become of him, he who has parted? / Waterfalls of the great mountain to leap, / the assemblage of great rain clouds thundering gives a sound.",
 "translation": "Even before the great, cool rains, the buds of the tender wild jasmine have turned deep red. Oh friend, I am bewildered like a frightened deer in the middle of the night. If he, who is now all alone, hears once again the thundering voice of the dark rain clouds that makes the waterfalls of the great mountain leap, what will become of him who parted from me?",
 "modern": "தோழியே! பெரிய குளிர்ந்த மழையினால் தளிர்விட்ட காட்டு மல்லிகையின் அரும்புகள் மழைக்கு முன்பே மிகச் சிவந்துவிட்டன. நள்ளிரவில் மான் போல நான் மருண்டு தவிக்கிறேன். அருவிகள் ஆர்ப்பரித்துக் கொட்டுமாறு பெரிய மலையில் மேகக்கூட்டங்கள் இடி முழக்கம் செய்யும் இந்த ஓசையை, என்னைப் பிரிந்து தனியாக இருக்கும் அவர் கேட்டால் மீண்டும் என்ன பாடுபடுவாரோ?",
 "explanation": "In this Mullai (pastoral) poem, the heroine expresses her deep anxiety to her friend during the onset of the rainy season. The rainy season is the designated time for the hero's return, but he has not yet arrived. Seeing the wild jasmine buds turning red even before the heavy rains begin, she feels bewildered and frightened like a deer at midnight. Her concern is not just for her own loneliness, but for her lover who is traveling alone in a foreign land. She wonders how he will endure the thunderous sound of the rain clouds echoing over the mountain waterfalls, which will surely remind him of their separation.",
